WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.260
FedRAMP baseline selection starts with impact thinking.

00:00:03.260 --> 00:00:07.250
Federal systems are categorized by considering the potential adverse effect of a loss of

00:00:07.250 --> 00:00:10.200
confidentiality, integrity, or availability.

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:13.200
Confidentiality asks what happens if information is disclosed.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:17.130
Integrity asks what happens if information or processing is changed improperly.

00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:21.275
Availability asks what happens if the service is disrupted or cannot be used when needed.

00:00:21.275 --> 00:00:25.300
Low, Moderate, and High are practical labels for different levels of potential harm.

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:29.250
Low-impact systems generally involve limited adverse effects if something goes wrong.

00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:33.695
Moderate-impact systems involve more serious operational, mission, financial, privacy,

00:00:33.695 --> 00:00:34.885
or trust consequences.

00:00:34.885 --> 00:00:39.080
High-impact systems are associated with severe or catastrophic consequences if

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:42.290
confidentiality, integrity, or availability is lost.

00:00:42.290 --> 00:00:45.160
These are risk concepts, not badges of maturity.

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:48.860
Baseline selection should reflect the federal data, agency mission, system context,

00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:50.053
and intended use case.

00:00:50.053 --> 00:00:53.960
A SaaS product used for simple collaboration may have a different risk profile from a

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:56.540
platform that supports critical agency operations.

00:00:56.540 --> 00:00:59.990
The same cloud product may also be used differently by different customers.

00:00:59.990 --> 00:01:04.015
That is why teams should avoid choosing a baseline only because it sounds easier, cheaper,

00:01:04.015 --> 00:01:06.420
or more prestigious. The practical question is,

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:09.865
'What kind of harm could reasonably result if this cloud service failed in a

00:01:09.865 --> 00:01:13.065
security-relevant way?' That answer should be informed by agency use,

00:01:13.065 --> 00:01:18.115
data sensitivity, dependencies, business process importance, and operational expectations.

00:01:18.115 --> 00:01:19.950
Once that risk picture is understood,

00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:23.820
the baseline becomes a structured way to plan the controls, evidence, assessment activity,

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:26.700
and monitoring needed to support the authorization decision.
